<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <parent>
        <groupId>com.vaadin.swingbridge</groupId>
        <artifactId>swingbridge-emulators-parent</artifactId>
        <version>0.1-SNAPSHOT</version>
        <relativePath>../../pom.xml</relativePath>
    </parent>

    <artifactId>swingbridge-emulators-jcalendar-1.4</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>
    <!-- name + description are NOT inherited from the parent POM, and Maven Central validates
         each published artifact's metadata independently. The starter-prototype status is
         stated here too: M1D_addon_starter_status lists every migrator-facing place that says
         it, and a Central listing is one of them. -->
    <name>SwingBridge Emulators add-on: JCalendar 1.4</name>
    <description>JCalendar 1.4's JDateChooser API on top of SwingBridge Emulators, so a migrated
        application keeps compiling after rewriting its com.toedter imports to vaadinx.toedter.
        A clean reimplementation over a Vaadin date picker, not a fork of the upstream sources,
        licensed LGPL-2.1 like the library whose API it reproduces.
        Starter prototype: shipped as-is at the coverage it has.</description>

    <!-- Third-party add-on: provides JCalendar 1.4's JDateChooser API on top of SB-Emulators, so a
         migrated app keeps compiling after `com.toedter.` -> `vaadinx.toedter.`.

         The two version slots carry different facts, and neither can express the other's.
         artifactId carries the UPSTREAM version: which API surface you get. A migrator on
         JCalendar 1.4 cannot use a port of a different line. <version> is SB-Emulators', and is this
         add-on's own release train — our fixes and improvements plus the emulator ABI it was
         built against — so swingbridge-emulators-jcalendar-1.4:0.4 supersedes swingbridge-emulators-jcalendar-1.4:0.1 for the very
         same JCalendar API. Ordinary Maven semantics on the half that evolves; the upstream
         half never does.

         This module is a clean REIMPLEMENTATION, not a fork: no upstream code is copied. It takes
         upstream's licence anyway, and the <licenses> block below overrides the parent's Apache-2.0
         (Maven replaces rather than merges). The rule is M1D_addon_upstream_licence and the reason
         is not caution for its own sake: LGPL-2.1 s3 permits relicensing an LGPL work to the
         ORDINARY GPLv2 and to nothing else — not to a permissive licence, and not to GPLv2 +
         Classpath Exception either, because the CE grants a linking permission over code that
         would not be ours. So an inherited licence is valid only in the future where this port
         never becomes derivative; LGPL-2.1 is valid in both. See PROVENANCE.md and LICENSE.

         Nothing in :emulators / :surrogates knows this module exists, and it must stay that
         way — a core change made to accommodate an add-on is the experiment failing. -->

    <licenses>
        <license>
            <name>GNU Lesser General Public License, Version 2.1</name>
            <url>https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.html</url>
            <distribution>repo</distribution>
            <comments>Reproduces the API of JCalendar 1.4 (com.toedter:jcalendar), which upstream
                licenses under the LGPL, and takes that licence rather than SB-Emulators' own.
                No upstream source or bytecode is copied. SPDX: LGPL-2.1-only. See LICENSE and
                PROVENANCE.md.</comments>
        </license>
    </licenses>

    <!-- PUBLISHED by a release build, like the library modules. The rule is D_kit_tool_zip: everything
         a migrated pom can be told to reference must be resolvable by coordinate, and this
         module's own MIGRATION.md tells an app to depend on it. The distribution kit does carry
         this jar, but only in tools/lib/ as a swap-table source for the import-swap tool — the
         migrated app resolves it by coordinate, so a coordinate that is not on Central has nothing
         left to fall back on. The licence lane is UPSTREAM's — LGPL-2.1, per
         M1D_addon_upstream_licence — which is also what closes the gate PROVENANCE.md used to
         record against publishing: the clearance to proceed on the reimplementation-is-not-
         derivative reading was given while this repository was private, and taking LGPL-2.1 makes
         the artifact correct whether or not that reading holds. Read PROVENANCE.md before cutting a
         release, and
         M1D_addon_starter_status is what states the starter-prototype scope to a migrator; that is
         a status to say out loud, not a reason to withhold the artifact. -->

    <dependencies>
        <!-- JDateChooser is an emulator-stage component: it extends vaadinx.swing.JComponent
             through the protected (Component peer) ctor. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.vaadin.swingbridge</groupId>
            <artifactId>swingbridge-emulators</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <!-- ...peering over SJFormattedDatePicker, plus the SHelper Date<->LocalDate seam. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.vaadin.swingbridge</groupId>
            <artifactId>swingbridge-surrogates</artifactId>
            <version>${project.version}</version>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.slf4j</groupId>
            <artifactId>slf4j-api</artifactId>
        </dependency>
        <!-- VaadinSession's interface references jakarta.servlet.http.HttpSessionBindingListener;
             needed to compile but provided by the container at runtime, as in :emulators. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>jakarta.servlet</groupId>
            <artifactId>jakarta.servlet-api</artifactId>
            <scope>provided</scope>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.slf4j</groupId>
            <artifactId>slf4j-simple</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter-engine</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.github.mvysny.kaributesting</groupId>
            <artifactId>karibu-testing-v24</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.platform</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-platform-launcher</artifactId>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <resources>
            <!-- Maven's default resource directory, restored: naming <resources> at all replaces
                 the default, and the swap table the import-swap tool reads lives here. Dropping it
                 leaves the table in the source tree but out of the jar, where the tool cannot find
                 it and silently rewrites nothing for this add-on's types. -->
            <resource>
                <directory>src/main/resources</directory>
            </resource>
            <!-- The migrator-facing addendum ships inside the jar, so an agent that resolved this
                 artifact by coordinate can read the migration instructions without a repo checkout
                 (and offline). Authored at the module root next to README/PROVENANCE; this block is
                 the only copy step. MigrationDocTest guards both halves. -->
            <resource>
                <directory>${project.basedir}</directory>
                <includes>
                    <include>MIGRATION.md</include>
                </includes>
                <targetPath>META-INF/emul</targetPath>
            </resource>
            <!-- This module's OWN LICENSE, not the repo root's — the same shape as the jgoodies
                 fork next door, and for a stronger reason than symmetry: shipping the parent's
                 Apache-2.0 text inside an LGPL-2.1 jar would state the wrong terms to whoever opens
                 it, and META-INF/LICENSE is the copy a jar travelling on its own carries. The root
                 PROVENANCE.md does not ship either: it is the record of SB-Emulators' OpenJDK
                 derivation, which is not this module's lane. This module's own PROVENANCE.md stays
                 a maintainer record and does not ship, as with the sibling fork.

                 NOTE this block replaces the parent's <resources> entirely, which is why the
                 default src/main/resources entry above has to be re-listed by hand. -->
            <resource>
                <directory>${project.basedir}</directory>
                <includes>
                    <include>LICENSE</include>
                </includes>
                <targetPath>META-INF</targetPath>
            </resource>
        </resources>
    </build>
</project>
